{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "{"id": 0, "depth": 0, "text": "(CNN)The Palestinian Authority officially became the 123rd member of the International Criminal Court on Wednesday, a step that gives the court jurisdiction over alleged crimes in Palestinian territories. The formal accession was marked with a ceremony at The Hague, in the Netherlands, where the court is based. The Palestinians signed the ICC's founding Rome Statute in January, when they also accepted its jurisdiction over alleged crimes committed \"in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, since June 13, 2014.\" Later that month, the ICC opened a preliminary examination into the situation in Palestinian territories, paving the way for possible war crimes investigations against Israelis. As members of the court, Palestinians may be subject to counter-charges as well. Israel and the United States, neither of which is an ICC member, opposed the Palestinians' efforts to join the body. But Palestinian Foreign Minister Riad al-Malki, speaking at Wednesday's ceremony, said it was a move toward greater justice. \"As Palestine formally becomes a State Party to the Rome Statute today, the world is also a step closer to ending a long era of impunity and injustice,\" he said, according to an ICC news release. \"Indeed, today brings us closer to our shared goals of justice and peace.\" Judge Kun", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "pythia-2.8b", "domain": "news"}
{"id": 1, "depth": 1, "text": "stler: The ICC is \"an idea that's been on the table for about a generation,\" says Bill Frezza, professor of law at John Jay College of Criminal Justice in New York and founder of the Law and Society Center in New York. He says the court is now \"in its adolescence\" and needs to attract states such as South Korea and Colombia as members. \"They may need to provide some assurances that their people will be protected against their own governments.\" Judge Kunstler, an activist and former US prosecutor, says the court could not function until the warring parties agree to cooperate and take action against those involved in abuses. \"If you want to keep the court alive, then you have to get states like South Korea and Colombia to join it.\" South Korea has declined to participate, but he thinks Colombia's government may consider joining in the near future. He also hopes South Africa would join at some point, and he thinks some Asian or European countries would be interested in joining the court. At present, the court is an \"archaic institution\" that is \"unsuited to modern times,\" he says. He says it might be time to think again about whether the court should be a permanent international institution or not. But if it is to be", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 256, "model": "pythia-2.8b", "domain": "news"}
