{"id": 11, "depth": 1, "text": " respond: \"Happy Hanukkah! Happy Rosh Hashanah is when you light the menorah! #Hesheesh,\" a jab at those who don't know the difference between a Jewish holiday and a Muslim one. . Zarif has an English major from Georgetown University with an impressive record as a reformist MP. . He once was a member of the Islamic Revolution Guards Corps. . His father is one of Iran's most prominent jurists. . His brother is an Iranian diplomat stationed in Iraq. . Zarif once served in the Iran-Iraq War. . Zarif's wife, Zahra Kazemi, is the daughter of a Persian-Armenian diplomat and the granddaughter of Iran's former minister of culture, Seyyed Kazem Khanom. . Zarif has been praised by former Iran nuclear negotiator Robert Ford and former U.S. ambassador John Negroponte. Zarif's \"Heshboni\" (\"Jovial\") nickname, according to the Iranian press, stems from the name of the ancient Jewish prophet Hanukah. And, just like in the Jewish holiday, the story is often about a light for the world. But, as with Hanukkah, there are", "seed_id": 2, "token_count": 256, "model": "pythia-2.8b", "domain": "news"}
{"id": 12, "depth": 2, "text": " other, darker implications.\n\nFor years, the Iranians have tried and failed to develop nuclear weapons. Iran, one of several nations, including Israel and Saudi Arabia, has pursued nuclear technology in part to produce weapons.\n\nOne of Iran's most senior nuclear negotiators, Hassan Rouhani, has said that his country is not seeking a nuclear arsenal. He said a nuclear weapon was not meant for Iran, but was aimed at countries like Israel, Pakistan and India.\n\n\"Iran does not pursue nuclear weapons; if it did, it wouldn't have nuclear technology in their hands,\" Rouhani said last month in an interview with \"60 Minutes.\"\n\nBut Iran's relationship with Israel remains the world's most complicated issue.\n\nZarif has often been involved in efforts to keep the two allies together.\n\n\"The two countries had a unique opportunity to establish a positive partnership. But the Iranian government squandered that opportunity,\" he said. \"The reason is that they failed to change the Iranian public's attitude regarding the Iranian nuclear issue.\n\n\"As long as Iran continues to support terrorists in this region and continues to support the spread of terror in the region, they will not achieve peace,\" he added.\n\nIn a", "seed_id": 2, "token_count": 256, "model": "pythia-2.8b", "domain": "news"}
